{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-07-path-parent-and-delegation-design.md","issuer":"mailto:benbarber121@gmail.com","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-07T17:54:34.374803Z","id":"1840d8eb20d1c47032016aee598f34055d6f7549c094eccb80d0aba2dd1d7d3e","body":{"detail":"An earlier draft of this design (toolpath PR #354 as first pushed, e63e8eb) gave path.parent a required relation field with values continuation and fork, enforced one continuation child per frozen parent path on the server (partial unique index, 409 continuation_exists), and had the writer declare the relation because derivers place trailing steps after a session's last turn so a resume could name an ancestor of the head. Dropped 2026-10-07 by the project owner: at the head a fork and a continuation are the same structural object, so the field recorded intent rather than fact; the trailing-step problem is the client's (a resume sets parent.step to the parent's head); and continuation vs fork are agent-coding-session vocabulary, not base-format facts. Now derived: continuation = parent.step is the parent path's head, fork = otherwise; several children at a head are all continuations.","kind":"alternative","suggested_fix":"Revisit if the stitched view needs one chosen continuation per frozen head. If so, carry the choice as mutable Pathbase metadata on the parent graph (owner-changeable pointer) or as a flag on the create/open request, never as a document field.","summary":"Declared relation on path.parent (continuation | fork) was considered and dropped","tags":["revisit:canonical-continuation","design","session:claude-code:e7a0e4d4-d193-4368-8056-1808f5a5eacd"]}}
